me that the bag contains a large number of candies and that the probability of selecting a red candy is the same on each trial."] [:db/cas 17592186411566 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of red candies.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ candies\n\n- the probability of getting a red candy is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red. This corresponds to $P(X<15).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X<15)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<15) & =1-P(X\\ge 15) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=15)+P(X=16)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{15})(0.7)^{15}(0.3)^{16-15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{16})(0.7)^{16}(0.3)^{16-16}] \\\\  & =1-[16(0.7)^{15}(0.3)+(0.7)^{16}] \\\\  & =1-0.026\\,112 \\\\  & =0.974\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of red candies.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ candies\n\n- the probability of getting a red candy is $p=70\\%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red. This corresponds to $P(X<15).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X<15)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<15) & =1-P(X\\ge 15) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=15)+P(X=16)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{15})(0.7)^{15}(0.3)^{16-15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{16})(0.7)^{16}(0.3)^{16-16}] \\\\  & =1-[16(0.7)^{15}(0.3)+(0.7)^{16}] \\\\  & =1-0.026\\,112 \\\\  & =0.974\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411569 :question/problem "A basketball player practices shooting $10$ free throws. The player has an $80%$ chance of making a basket in each free throw. What is the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A basketball player practices shooting $10$ free throws. The player has an $80\\%$ chance of making a basket in each free throw. What is the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411569 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of baskets that the player makes.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ free throws\n\n- the probability of making a basket in each free throw is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.8)^{9}(0.2)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.8)^{10}(0.2)^{0} \\\\  & =10(0.8)^{9}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{10} \\\\  & =0.376\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of baskets that the player makes.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ free throws\n\n- the probability of making a basket in each free throw is $p=80\\%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.8)^{9}(0.2)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.8)^{10}(0.2)^{0} \\\\  & =10(0.8)^{9}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{10} \\\\  & =0.376\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411570 :question/problem "A drone has a $65%$ chance of successfully delivering a package. It makes $5$ deliveries in one day. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it succeeds at most $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A drone has a $65\\%$ chance of successfully delivering a package. It makes $5$ deliveries in one day. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it succeeds at most $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186411570 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful deliveries.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ deliveries.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=65%=0.65.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.65).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(1-0.65)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(0.35)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the drone succeeds at most $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.65)^{4}(0.35)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.65)^{5}(0.35)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.65)^{4}(0.35)+(0.65)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.428\\,415 \\\\  & ≈0.572,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful deliveries.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ deliveries.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=65\\%=0.65.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.65).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(1-0.65)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(0.35)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the drone succeeds at most $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.65)^{4}(0.35)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.65)^{5}(0.35)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.65)^{4}(0.35)+(0.65)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.428\\,415 \\\\  & ≈0.572,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411571 :question/problem "$5%$ of electric bulbs are defective at manufacture. If six bulbs are randomly tested, with each one being replaced before the next is chosen, what is the probability that at least two are defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "$5\\%$ of electric bulbs are defective at manufacture. If six bulbs are randomly tested, with each one being replaced before the next is chosen, what is the probability that at least two are defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411571 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of defective tested bulbs. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ tested bulbs\n\n- the probability of choosing a defective bulb is $p=5%=0.05$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.05).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(1-0.05)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(0.95)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least two bulbs are defective. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{6-1}] \\\\  & =1-[1(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6}+6(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.967\\,226 \\\\  & ≈0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of defective tested bulbs. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ tested bulbs\n\n- the probability of choosing a defective bulb is $p=5\\%=0.05$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.05).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(1-0.05)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(0.95)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least two bulbs are defective. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{6-1}] \\\\  & =1-[1(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6}+6(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.967\\,226 \\\\  & ≈0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411575 :question/problem "A basketball player has a $50%$ chance of making a free throw. If they take $7$ shots, what is the probability that they make at most $3$ of them?\n\nThe probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A basketball player has a $50\\%$ chance of making a free throw. If they take $7$ shots, what is the probability that they make at most $3$ of them?\n\nThe probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186411575 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ shots.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=50%=0.5.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.5).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $3$ or fewer of the $7$ shots are successful. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & ={\\sum }_{x=0}^{3}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})](0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =(1+7+21+35)\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ shots.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=50\\%=0.5.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.5).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $3$ or fewer of the $7$ shots are successful. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & ={\\sum }_{x=0}^{3}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})](0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =(1+7+21+35)\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186411576 :question/problem "On a particular webpage, there is an $80%$ probability that each downloaded file does not contain a computer virus. If $6$ files are downloaded, what is the probability that at least $5$ of the files are virus-free? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "On a particular webpage, there is an $80\\%$ probability that each downloaded file does not contain a computer virus. If $6$ files are downloaded, what is the probability that at least $5$ of the files are virus-free? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411576 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of virus-free files.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ files\n\n- the probability that each file is virus-free is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $5$ of the $6$ files are virus-free. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.8)^{5}(0.2)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.8)^{5}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & =0.655\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of virus-free files.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ files\n\n- the probability that each file is virus-free is $p=80\\%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $5$ of the $6$ files are virus-free. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.8)^{5}(0.2)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.8)^{5}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & =0.655\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186415881 :question/problem "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60\\%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415881 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}\\right) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}\\right) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22\\%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415882 :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the videos produced by a popular content creator is $35\\,\\text{min}.$ Also, $5%$ of their videos have a duration of less than $20$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of the videos is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the duration times, rounded to the near minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" "The average duration (in minutes) of the videos produced by a popular content creator is $35\\,\\text{min}.$ Also, $5\\%$ of their videos have a duration of less than $20$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of the videos is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the duration times, rounded to the near minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186418459 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four bills is drawn from a population. The population has bills of two different denominations, $$5$ and $$10,$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four bills is drawn from a population. The population has bills of two different denominations, $\\$5$ and $\\$10,$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186418459 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bills in the population have denominations $$5$ and $$10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bills only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$5,\\,$5,\\,$5,\\,$10\\,$10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(15+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =$7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {5}^{2}+2\\cdot {10}^{2})-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(275)-49 \\\\  & =55-49 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5.\n\n$$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{1.5}≈$1.22.\n$$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bills in the population have denominations $\\$5$ and $\\$10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bills only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$5,\\,$5,\\,$5,\\,$10\\,$10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(15+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =$7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {5}^{2}+2\\cdot {10}^{2})-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(275)-49 \\\\  & =55-49 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5.\n\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{1.5}≈$1.22.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186420371 :question/problem "A student who answers randomly has a $50%$ chance of correctly answering each question on a $12$-question test. What is the probability that the student selects exactly $10$ correct answers? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A student who answers randomly has a $50\\%$ chance of correctly answering each question on a $12$-question test. What is the probability that the student selects exactly $10$ correct answers? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186420371 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of correct answers that the student gets.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ questions\n\n- the probability of hitting each question is $p=50%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.5).$ We want to find the probability of getting $x=10$ correct answers.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{10})(0.5)^{10}(1-0.5)^{12-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{10!(12-10)!}(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =66(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.016\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of correct answers that the student gets.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ questions\n\n- the probability of hitting each question is $p=50\\%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.5).$ We want to find the probability of getting $x=10$ correct answers.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{10})(0.5)^{10}(1-0.5)^{12-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{10!(12-10)!}(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =66(0.5)^{10}(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.016\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186420372 :question/problem "An unfair coin has an $80%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $5$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that the coin landed on heads at least four times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "An unfair coin has an $80\\%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $5$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that the coin landed on heads at least four times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186420372 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=80%=0.8.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the coin landed on heads at least $4$ of the $5$ games. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.8)^{4}(1-0.8)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.8)^{5}(1-0.8)^{5-5} \\\\  & =5(0.8)^{4}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.737,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places" "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=80\\%=0.8.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random 