 and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of detecting an animal at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=7)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{7-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{7}+7(0.4)(0.6)^{6}+21(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.580,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the camera successfully detected an animal.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ triggers.\n\n- The probability of detection is $p=40\\%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of detecting an animal at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=7)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{7-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{7}+7(0.4)(0.6)^{6}+21(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.580,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186421987 :question/problem "A tennis player has a $60%$ chance of winning each match in a $5$-match tournament. What is the mean number of matches that the player wins?" "A tennis player has a $60\\%$ chance of winning each match in a $5$-match tournament. What is the mean number of matches that the player wins?"] [:db/cas 17592186421987 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of matches that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ matches\n\n- the probability of winning each match is $p=60%=0.6$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.6).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of matches that the player wins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(5)(0.60) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of matches that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ matches\n\n- the probability of winning each match is $p=60\\%=0.6$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.6).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of matches that the player wins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(5)(0.60) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186421990 :question/problem "A new drug has a $64%$ probability of curing a patient within a week. If $50$ patients are treated, what is the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A new drug has a $64\\%$ probability of curing a patient within a week. If $50$ patients are treated, what is the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186421990 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug has cured a patient within a week is $p=64%=0.64$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.64).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(1-0.64)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(0.36)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11.52} \\\\  & ≈3.394\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug has cured a patient within a week is $p=64\\%=0.64$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.64).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(1-0.64)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(0.36)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11.52} \\\\  & ≈3.394\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186421991 :question/problem "An archer practices shooting $16$ arrows at a target. The archer has a $25%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. What is the variance in the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye?" "An archer practices shooting $16$ arrows at a target. The archer has a $25\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. What is the variance in the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye?"] [:db/cas 17592186421991 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ shots\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=25%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of arrows hitting the bull's eye is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(16)(0.25)(1-0.25) \\\\  & =(4)(0.75) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ shots\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=25\\%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, a