ven by:\n\n|  |  | $2{x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186434387 :question/problem "Select the options that make the statement below true.\n\n$\\,$\n$45%$ is equivalent to \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{17}{40}.$" "Select the options that make the statement below true.\n\n$\\,$\n$45\\%$ is equivalent to \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{17}{40}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186434387 :question/worked-solution "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $45%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n45%=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $40$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 2}{20\\times 2}=\\frac{18}{40}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{40}>\\frac{17}{40}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{18}{40},$ \nwhich is $\\text{greater than}$ $\\frac{17}{40}.$" "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $45\\%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n45\\%=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $40$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 2}{20\\times 2}=\\frac{18}{40}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{40}>\\frac{17}{40}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{18}{40},$ \nwhich is $\\text{greater than}$ $\\frac{17}{40}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186434388 :question/problem "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45\\%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186434388 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early” is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia” is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince  $40%<45%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” only." "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early” is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45\\%<50\\%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia” is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45\\%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40\\%\n$$\n\nSince  $40\\%<45\\%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” only."] [:db/cas 17592186435596 :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186435653 :question/problem "A quality‑check sensor detects a flaw with probability $40%.$ If $5$ items are inspected, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $2$ of them contain a flaw?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A quality‑check sensor detects a flaw with probability $40\\%.$ If $5$ items are inspected, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $2$ of them contain a flaw?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186435653 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of flawed items detected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ items.\n\n- The probability of a flaw is $p=40%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $5$ items contain flaws. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4}+10(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.683,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of flawed items detected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ items.\n\n- The probability of a flaw is $p=40\\%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $5$ items contain flaws. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4}+10(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.683,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435654 :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $20$ times. If the archer has a $20%$ chance of hitting the bullseye, what is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $20$ times. If the archer has a $20\\%$ chance of hitting the bullseye, what is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435654 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the arrow hits the bullseye. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ trials\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye is $p=20%=0.2$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{20}+20(0.2)(0.8)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.069\\,175 \\\\  & =0.931\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the arrow hits the bullseye. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ trials\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye is $p=20\\%=0.2$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{20}+20(0.2)(0.8)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.069\\,175 \\\\  & =0.931\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435655 :question/problem "An unfair coin has a $15%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "An unfair coin has a $15\\%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186435655 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=15%=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.15).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{10}+10(0.15)(0.85)^{9}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.544\\,300 \\\\  & ≈0.456,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=15\\%=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.15).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{10}+10(0.15)(0.85)^{9}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.544\\,300 \\\\  & ≈0.456,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435656 :question/problem "In a coffee shop, $28%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "In a coffee shop, $28\\%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435656 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=28%=0.28$\n\nSo, 