\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186441672 :question/problem "$70%$ of $80$ is equal to{{field-1}}." "$70\\%$ of $80$ is equal to{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186441672 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $80,$ and the percentage is $70%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70%=70÷100=0.7\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70%\\times 80 \\\\  & =0.7\\times 80 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70%$ of $80$ is $56.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n70%=\\frac{70}{100}=\\frac{70÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\times 80 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{80}{10} \\\\  & =7\\times 8 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70%$ of $80$ is $56.$" "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $80,$ and the percentage is $70\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70\\%=70÷100=0.7\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70\\%\\times 80 \\\\  & =0.7\\times 80 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70\\%$ of $80$ is $56.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=\\frac{70÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70\\%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\times 80 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{80}{10} \\\\  & =7\\times 8 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70\\%$ of $80$ is $56.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441673 :question/problem "$225%$ of $32$ is equal to{{field-1}}." "$225\\%$ of $32$ is equal to{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186441673 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $32,$ and the percentage is $225%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225%=225÷100=2.25\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =225%\\times 32 \\\\  & =2.25\\times 32 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $225%$ of $32$ is $72.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n225%=\\frac{225}{100}=\\frac{225÷25}{100÷25}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =225%\\times 32 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\times 32 \\\\  & =9\\times \\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =9\\times 8 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $225%$ of $32$ is $72.$" "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $32,$ and the percentage is $225\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225\\%=225÷100=2.25\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =225\\%\\times 32 \\\\  & =2.25\\times 32 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $225\\%$ of $32$ is $72.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n225\\%=\\frac{225}{100}=\\frac{225÷25}{100÷25}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =225\\%\\times 32 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\times 32 \\\\  & =9\\times \\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =9\\times 8 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $225\\%$ of $32$ is $72.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441674 :question/problem "$0.4%$ of $750$ is equal to{{field-1}}." "$0.4\\%$ of $750$ is equal to{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186441674 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $750,$ and the percentage is $0.4%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n0.4%=0.4÷100=0.004\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4%\\times 750 \\\\  & =0.004\\times 750 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4%$ of $750$ is $3.$ \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n0.4%=\\frac{0.4}{100}=\\frac{0.4\\times 10}{100\\times 10}=\\frac{4}{1,000}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4%\\times 750 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1,000}\\times 750 \\\\  & =\\frac{3,000}{1,000} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4%$ of $750$ is $3.$" "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $750,$ and the percentage is $0.4\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n0.4\\%=0.4÷100=0.004\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4\\%\\times 750 \\\\  & =0.004\\times 750 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4\\%$ of $750$ is $3.$ \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n0.4\\%=\\frac{0.4}{100}=\\frac{0.4\\times 10}{100\\times 10}=\\frac{4}{1,000}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4\\%\\times 750 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1,000}\\times 750 \\\\  & =\\frac{3,000}{1,000} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4\\%$ of $750$ is $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441675 :question/problem "What is $0.4%$ of $150?$" "What is $0.4\\%$ of $150?$"] [:db/cas 17592186441675 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $150,$ and the percentage is $0.4%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n0.4%=0.4÷100=0.004\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4%\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.004\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4%$ of $150$ is $0.6.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n0.4%=\\frac{0.4}{100}=\\frac{0.4\\times 10}{100\\times 10}=\\frac{4}{1,000}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4%\\times 150 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1,000}\\times 150 \\\\  & =\\frac{600}{1,000} \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4%$ of $150$ is $0.6.$" "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $150,$ and the percentage is $0.4\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n0.4\\%=0.4÷100=0.004\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.4\\%\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.004\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4\\%$ of $1