 given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(50)(0.75)(1-0.75) \\\\  & =(50)(0.75)(0.25) \\\\  & =9.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455431 :question/problem "Last year, a factory produced $800$ bicycles. This year, production is $150%$ greater than last year. How many bicycles did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles this year." "Last year, a factory produced $800$ bicycles. This year, production is $150\\%$ greater than last year. How many bicycles did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles this year."] [:db/cas 17592186455431 :question/worked-solution "To find this year's bicycle production, we apply a $150%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $150%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+150%=250%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250%=\\frac{250}{100}=2.5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n800\\times 2.5=2,000\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $2000$ bicycles this year." "To find this year's bicycle production, we apply a $150\\%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $150\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+150\\%=250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\%=\\frac{250}{100}=2.5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n800\\times 2.5=2,000\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $2000$ bicycles this year."] [:db/cas 17592186455444 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%\\times 25=40\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=40÷25=\\frac{40