/cas 17592186455493 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%\\times 14=35\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=35÷14=\\frac{35}{14}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{14}=\\frac{35÷7}{14÷7}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5\\times 50}{2\\times 50}=\\frac{250}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{250}{100}=250%\n$$" "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%\\times 14=35\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=35÷14=\\frac{35}{14}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{14}=\\frac{35÷7}{14÷7}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5\\times 50}{2\\times 50}=\\frac{250}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{250}{100}=250\\%\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455495 :question/problem "A museum admission costs $$24$ before tax. If an $8.75%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price of the admission after tax?" "A museum admission costs $\\$24$ before tax. If an $8.75\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price of the admission after tax?"] [:db/cas 17592186455495 :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $8.75%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $8.75%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+8.75%=108.75%=1.0875\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0875:$\n\n$$\n24\\times 1.0875=26.1.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the admission after tax is $$26.10.$" "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $8.75\\%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $8.75\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+8.75\\%=108.75\\%=1.0875\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0875:$\n\n$$\n24\\times 1.0875=26.1.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the admission after tax is $\\$26.10.$"] [:db/cas 17592186455498 :question/problem "A pair of shoes costs $$32$ before tax. If a $7.5%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?" "A pair of shoes costs $\\$32$ before tax. If a $7.5\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?"] [:db/cas 17592186455498 :question/worked-solution "To find the total cost after tax, we apply a $7.5%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.5%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+7.5%=107.5%=1.075\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.075:$\n\n$$\n32\\times 1.075=34.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $$34.40.$" "To find the total cost after tax, we apply a $7.5\\%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+7.5\\%=107.5\\%=1.075\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.075:$\n\n$$\n32\\times 1.075=34.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $\\$34.40.$"] [:db/cas 17592186455511 :question/problem "An artist painted $60$ portraits last year. This year, her output is $400%$ greater than last year. How many portraits did the artist paint this year?\n\nThe artist painted\n{{field-1}}\nportraits this year." "An artist painted $60$ portraits last year. This year, her output is $400\\%$ greater than last year. How many portraits did the artist paint this year?\n\nThe artist painted\n{{field-1}}\nportraits this year."] [:db/cas 17592186455511 :question/worked-solution "To find this year's portrait output, we apply a $400%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+400%=500%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500%=\\frac{500}{100}=5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5:$\n\n$$\n60\\times 5=300\n$$\n\nSo, the artist painted $300$ portraits this year." "To find this year's portrait output, we apply a $400\\%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+400\\%=500\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500\\%=\\frac{500}{100}=5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5:$\n\n$$\n60\\times 5=300\n$$\n\nSo, the artist painted $300$ portraits this year."] [:db/cas 17592186459213 :question/problem "David saved $$500$ to buy a new cellphone. If he spent only $$420$ to buy the cellphone, what percentage of David's savings did he spend on the phone?" "David saved $\\$500$ to buy a new cellphone. If he spent only $\\$420$ to buy the cellphone, what percentage of David's savings did he spend on the phone?"] [:db/cas 17592186459213 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the money David spent, which is $420,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total amount of money saved, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=420÷500=\\frac{42}{50}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{50}=\\frac{42\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{84}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{84}{100}=84%\n$$\n\nTherefore, David spent $84%$ of the total money saved." "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the money David spent, which is $420,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total amount of money saved, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=420÷500=\\frac{42}{50}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{50}=\\frac{42\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{84}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{84}{100}=84\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, David spent $84\\%$ of the total money saved."] [:db/cas 17592186459256 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of episodes Ashley has watched, which is $8,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of episodes, which is $50.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=8÷50=\\frac{8}{50}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{50}=\\frac{8\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{100}=16%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ashley has watched $16%$ of the TV series." "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of episodes Ashley has watched, which is $8,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of episodes, which is $50.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=8÷50=\\frac{8}{50}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{50}=\\frac{8\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{100}=16\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ashley has watched $16\\%$ of the TV series."] [:db/cas 17592186459298 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of clubs in the deck, which is $13,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of cards in the deck, which is $52.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=13÷52=\\frac{13}{52}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{13÷13}{52÷13}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=25%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25%$ of the poker deck are clubs." "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of clubs in the deck, which is $13,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of cards in the deck, which is $52.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=13÷52=\\frac{13}{52}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{13÷13}{52÷13}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=25\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25\\%$ of the poker deck are clubs."] [:db/cas 17592186459303 :question/problem "Dan purchased his land for $160$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Dan's plot be worth after $5$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." "Dan purchased his land for $160$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10\\%$ each year. How much will Dan's plot be worth after $5$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186459303 :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $160$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $5$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{5}$ thousand dollars\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $5$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n160\\cdot (1.1)^{5}=257.68≈258\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately $258$ thousand dollars." "The land initially costs $160$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10\\%$ per year.\n\n$10\\%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $5$ years, the value will be $160\\cdot (1.1)^{5}$ thousand dollars\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $5$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n160\\cdot (1.1)^{5}=257.68≈258\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately $258$ thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186459309 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of minutes George runs, which is $54,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of minutes George exercises, which is $120.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=54÷120=\\frac{54}{120}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{54}{120}=\\frac{54÷6}{120÷6}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=45%\n$$\n\nTherefore, George runs for $45%$ of his overall training time." "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of minutes George runs, which is $54,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of minutes George exercises, which is $120.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=54÷120=\\frac{54}{120}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{54}{120}=\\frac{54÷6}{120÷6}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=45\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, George runs for $45\\%$ of his overall training time."] [: