/tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$b$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$c$</td><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186476172 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are $3$ arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there are three routes from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=3.$\n\n- There are $3$ arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are three routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=3.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$1$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+3+1=7.\n$$" "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are $3$ arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there are three routes from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=3.$\n\n- There are $3$ arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are three routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=3.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$1$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+3+1=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478392 :question/problem "The price of crude oil, measured in dollars per barrel, increases at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{2}{1+3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?" "The price of crude oil, measured in dollars per barrel, increases at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{2}{1+3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $\\$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?"] [:db/cas 17592186478411 :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $$46\\,000.$" "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $\\$46\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186480651 :question/problem "Fred purchased his land for $200$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Fred's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." "Fred purchased his land for $200$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10\\%$ each year. How much will Fred's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186480651 :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $200$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}=292.82≈293,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $293$ thousand dollars." "The land initially costs $200$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10\\%$ per year.\n\n$10\\%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}=292.82≈293,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $293$ thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186483369 :question/problem "Orlando bought $6$ packages of powdered fertilizer, for which he paid $$96.44.$ Estimate the price of each package by rounding the amount of money he spent to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price of each package is $\n{{field-1}}." "Orlando bought $6$ packages of powdered fertilizer, for which he paid $\\$96.44.$ Estimate the price of each package by rounding the amount of money he spent to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price of each package is \\$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186483369 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the price of each package, we need to find an approximation to $96.44÷6.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n96.44\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $6$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷6=16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each package is $$16.$" "To estimate the price of each package, we need to find an approximation to $96.44÷6.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n96.44\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $6$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷6=16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each package is $\\$16.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485497 :question/problem "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $90%$ of those infected and $9%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $14%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places." "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $90\\%$ of those infected and $9\\%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $14\\%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186485497 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $90%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $9%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.9=0.1,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.09=0.91.\n$$\n\n- Since only $14%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.14\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.14=0.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.91)(0.86)}{(0.1)(0.14)+(0.91)(0.86)} \\\\  & ≈0.9824\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded four decimal places." "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $90\\%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $9\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.9=0.1,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.09=0.91.\n$$\n\n- Since only $14\\%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.14\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.14=0.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.91)(0.86)}{(0.1)(0.14)+(0.91)(0.86)} \\\\  & ≈0.9824\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded four decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186485500 :question/problem "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke. $51%$ of the population are female. If a non-smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being female? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $17\\%$ of all females and  $38\\%$ of all males smoke. $51\\%$ of the population are female. If a non-smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being female? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186485500 :question/worked-solution "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't smoke, and let $F$ be the event that they are female. Then, we require $P(F|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|N)=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|F)=1-0.17=0.83,\\,P(N|{F}^{′})=1-0.38=0.62.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.51,\\,P({F}^{′})=1-0.51=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|N) & =\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.83)(0.51)}{(0.83)(0.51)+(0.62)(0.49)} \\\\  & ≈58%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't smoke, and let $F$ be the event that they are female. Then, we require $P(F|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|N)=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $17\\%$ of all females and  $38\\%$ of all males smoke, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|F)=1-0.17=0.83,\\,P(N|{F}^{′})=1-0.38=0.62.\n$$\n\n- Since $51\\%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.51,\\,P({F}^{′})=1-0.51=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|N) & =\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.83)(0.51)}{(0.83)(0.51)+(0.62)(0.49)} \\\\  & ≈58\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186485502 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3{x}^{2}-5x+2$ | __ |  |  | $3{x}^{2}-5x+2$ |" "Express $\\frac{18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.\n\n| $18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| ---