 the baseline chose yoga."] [:db/cas 17592186486392 :question/problem "What number is $125%$ greater than $64?$" "What number is $125\\%$ greater than $64?$"] [:db/cas 17592186486392 :question/worked-solution "To find a number $125%$ *greater than* $64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+125%=225%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225%=\\frac{225}{100}=2.25\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $125%$ greater than $64,$ we multiply $64$ by $2.25:$\n\n$$\n2.25\\cdot 64=144\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $125%$ greater than $64$ is $144.$" "To find a number $125\\%$ *greater than* $64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+125\\%=225\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225\\%=\\frac{225}{100}=2.25\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $125\\%$ greater than $64,$ we multiply $64$ by $2.25:$\n\n$$\n2.25\\cdot 64=144\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $125\\%$ greater than $64$ is $144.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486393 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186486393 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60%=0.6,\\,n=140.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(140,0.6)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =84 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.6\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =33.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has 