\\%=145\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145\\%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n3,\\,500,\\,000\\times 1.45=5,\\,075,\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of tourists in $2028$ is $5,\\,075,\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486446 :question/problem "Suppose that $60%$ of the child population enrolled in a baseball academy is right-handed. A random sample of $n=20$ children is taken from the population and asked about their dominant hand.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of right-handed children in the sample? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose that $60\\%$ of the child population enrolled in a baseball academy is right-handed. A random sample of $n=20$ children is taken from the population and asked about their dominant hand.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of right-handed children in the sample? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186486452 :question/problem "Find $110%$ of $80,$ and then $30%$ of the result." "Find $110\\%$ of $80,$ and then $30\\%$ of the result."] [:db/cas 17592186486452 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $110%$ of $80:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110%\\times 80 & =\\frac{110}{100}\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.1\\times 80 \\\\  & =88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $30%$ of the result $(88):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30%\\times 88 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 88 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 88 \\\\  & =26.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $26.4.$" "First, we find $110\\%$ of $80:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110\\%\\times 80 & =\\frac{110}{100}\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.1\\times 80 \\\\  & =88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $30\\%$ of the result $(88):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\%\\times 88 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 88 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 88 \\\\  & =26.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $26.4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486457 :question/problem "Suppose that $25%$ of a high school student population like mathematics. A random sample of $n=25$ high school students is taken from the population and asked about their favorite subject.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who like mathematics? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "Suppose that $25\\%$ of a high school student population like mathematics. A random sample of $n=25$ high school students is taken from the population and asked about their favorite subject.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who like mathematics? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186486463 :question/problem "It is known that  $5%$ of the researchers in a particular university work in neurobiology. A random sample of $n=15$ researchers is taken from the population and asked about their work area.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of researchers in the sample working in neurobiology? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "It is known that  $5\\%$ of the researchers in a particular university work in neurobiology. A random sample of $n=15$ researchers is taken from the population and asked about their work area.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of researchers in the sample working in neurobiology? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186487295 :question/problem "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two light bulb types: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $2%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $50%$ of the entire production, and $3%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, and $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two light bulb types: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $25\\%$ of the entire production, and $2\\%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $50\\%$ of the entire production, and $3\\%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $25\\%$ of the entire production, and $1\\%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $40\\%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, and $1\\%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186487295 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1\\%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186487319 :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer print handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove her belief, she puts some students into three groups according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in print.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $55%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $52%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\nIf $42%$ of students are female, $80%$ of female students write in print, and $56%$ of students that write in print are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "An English teacher believes that students who prefer print handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove her belief, she puts some students into three groups according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in print.\n\n- $20\\%$ of students have low grades.\n\n- $65\\%$ of students have average grades, and $55\\%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\n- $15\\%$ of students have high grades, and $52\\%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\nIf $42\\%$ of students are female, $80\\%$ of female students write in print, and $56\\%$ of students that write in print are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186487319 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that a student writes in print, and $L,A,H$ be the event that a student has low, average, or high writing grades, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.55 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females write in print, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students that write in print are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|W)=0.56.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print, which is represented as $P(W|L).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W|L),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56(0.2P(W|L)+0.4355) & =0.336 \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.56} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L) & =\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355 \\\\ P(W|L) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355) \\\\ P(W|L) & ≈0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that a student writes in print, and $L,A,H$ be the event that a student has low, average, or high writing grades, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.55 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42\\%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $80\\%$ of females write in print, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $56\\%$ of students that write in print are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|W)=0.56.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print, which is represented as $P(W|L).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W|L),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56(0.2P(W|L)+0.4355) & =0.336 \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.56} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L) & =\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355 \\\\ P(W|L) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355) \\\\ P(W|L) & ≈0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."]]
